{"retractions" [[17592186321097 :question/worked-solution "For real $x$ and $y$, each square is nonnegative, so $x^2+y^2\\ge 0$. Their sum equals zero exactly when $x=y=0$. Consequently,\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2\\ne 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2>0.\n$$\n\nThe logarithm in $g(x,y)=x\\ln(x^2+y^2)$ requires $x^2+y^2>0$. This same condition makes the denominators in both supplied partial derivatives nonzero. On this open set the logarithm and rational terms are differentiable, so both partial derivatives exist. At the origin the original function is undefined, so its partial derivatives do not exist there.\n\nThus the domain where all partial derivatives exist is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2>0\\}=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nThe choices using $x^2+y^2\\ne 0$ (c) and $x^2+y^2>0$ (e) describe exactly the same domain. The question explicitly requests the strict positivity form, so choose e. Choice c is an equivalent description of the domain, but it does not express the condition as the requested positivity inequality."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186321099 17592186321100 17592186321101 17592186321102 17592186321103] "immutable_fields" [[17592186321372 :answer-field/id] [17592186321372 :answer-field/key] [17592186321372 :answer-field/type] [17592186321372 :answer-field/correct] [17592186321372 :answer-field/choices]]}
